WEBVTT

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.000
Imagine you're a container.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.110
You came off one ship this morning,

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:21.390
and you have forty-one
minutes to make the next one.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.670
Miss it, and you wait for the next sailing.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.140
And nobody at the terminal
knows you're in danger,

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.080
because the data feed every
system watches has gone quiet.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.340
On two recorded days in Singapore,

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.540
sixty-three of seventy-four late ships
gave that feed no warning at all.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.380
The warning did exist, though.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.800
It was sitting in an email, and
no rules engine reads email.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.900
That gap is what RELAY is built for.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.980
To see why the feed was
quiet, look at the two lanes.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.315
The top lane is the structured feed,

00:00:51.315 --> 00:00:58.850
and on those two days 463 of its 1,106
arrival estimates were already in the past

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.050
when they were sent.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:03.440
The bottom lane is the carrier's email,
where the carrier often knows first,

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.740
so when the feed is silent, the
email is the earlier signal.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:14.030
PSA keeps 84% of its assured calls on
time; the rest is where RELAY works.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.340
So where does the email go?

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.710
Follow the token across the diagram.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:23.205
It goes to the language model first,

00:01:23.205 --> 00:01:25.290
which turns the free text into a checked fact.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.170
The twin, our simulator of the terminal,

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.920
recomputes the minutes the box has left.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.620
The solver picks the cheapest recovery.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:36.880
A policy table the model cannot
edit decides who gets asked.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.000
And the duty officer decides
what actually gets written.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.530
The model never picks the
tool, the tier or the action.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.230
Let's start with that email.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:51.350
On the console, the advisory arrives
and the trace records the model's call.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:57.000
A small model reading alone invents
an arrival time in 47.7% of the cases

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.220
where there is none,

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.780
so RELAY samples it three times,

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:05.610
five if they disagree, and the
parses vote field by field.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:07.830
Only allow-listed keys survive,

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:11.090
and a port the email
never mentions is dropped.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:14.280
What remains is a fact
with a completeness score,

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:21.460
0.87 against a gate of 0.60; below
the gate, a person is told instead.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:26.970
And in 500 seeded scenarios, 35
connections were at risk only in an email:

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:32.200
rules saw none, and the agent
escalated all 35 to a person.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.650
Once the fact is in, the twin takes over.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:39.620
It computes margin at the ninetieth
percentile of yard move times,

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.300
so feasible means feasible on a bad day,

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.360
and for our box that margin
is 41 minutes: at risk.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:51.150
Then come the options: expedite
the yard transfer for $800;

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:54.520
ask the carrier to extend
the cut-off for nothing,

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:59.450
although a request is not a
grant; or rebook for $2,400.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:02.030
And when one late ship
breaks three connections,

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:03.940
they share one shift budget,

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:09.890
so a solver allocates one action to
each in a single solve, $5,600 in total.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.810
The solver wants an expedite.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.120
Can it just do it?

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:15.320
No.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.580
Every action the agent can
propose has a row in it,

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.210
and the row sets the tier.

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:21.820
An expedite is row 3,
tier T1: ask and approve.

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:24.370
So a card is minted for the duty officer,

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.420
with a single-use token bound
to these exact arguments,

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.800
and a 120-second clock starts.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.790
If nobody answers, silence counts as no,

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:36.370
and that default has a
cost we will come back to.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.920
Now watch the officer work on the card.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:44.500
First, a question to the
twin: what would a rebook do?

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.220
Then the officer raises
the expedite to CRITICAL:

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.760
the policy row moves from 3 to 4,

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.680
the risk becomes high, and a written
justification becomes mandatory.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.620
The officer types one,

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:00.490
and behind the button the token is
re-minted over the edited arguments,

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:04.290
so the write that executes
is exactly what was approved.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:05.490
Approve.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:08.685
On the board, the margin
goes from 41 minutes to 101,

00:04:08.685 --> 00:04:11.845
and the first card sits
underneath, marked denied.

00:04:11.845 --> 00:04:14.900
This take was recorded
with the pricing gate off;

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.700
under the shipped default
this expedite would be advice.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:22.230
The approved write now meets a gate,

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:27.130
which runs four checks the way a bank
checks a cheque: is the token real

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:31.330
and bound to these arguments, is
the caller allowed to present it,

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:35.540
has this write already happened,
and is the episode degraded.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:39.090
The striped band behind the
gate is the fault layer,

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:43.250
and the gate runs before it, so a
bypassed fault cannot skip the checks.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:45.740
We attacked this path fourteen times;

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:48.830
14 of 14 are refused, after four fixes.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:51.230
So far everything worked.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.080
What happens when it does not?

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:54.740
One click on the fault control,

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.270
and the header flips to degraded to advisory.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:01.465
Try to approve a second card,
and the server refuses the write.

00:05:01.465 --> 00:05:02.810
Now leave a card unanswered.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:05.900
The window was shortened
for the recording, and

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:11.630
when it runs out the card expires into deny by
default with a written summary to the duty supervisor.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:14.350
Last, from the command line, so staged,

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:18.390
we fire a pack whose cheapest
remedy has no row in the table;

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:22.300
it falls to row 10 and is
denied before a card can exist.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:25.860
Each time the default is no,
and a person hears about it.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:30.560
Every step you have
watched went onto a ledger,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.020
and each event hashes the one before.

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:35.130
So what if someone edits history?

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:38.640
Change one byte of an old event,
and every hash after it breaks.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:41.980
Shorten the chain, and the
head anchor catches it.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:46.300
On the console the chip turns to
chain broken and replay is refused.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:49.125
Tamper-evident, then, not tamper-proof:

00:05:49.125 --> 00:05:52.220
someone with root access
could still rewrite it,

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:57.250
which is why production keeps the
store outside the agent's reach.

00:05:57.250 --> 00:06:03.160
That is a lot of controls, so how
do we know each one is wired in?

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:05.090
Green tests cannot tell you.

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:09.075
So we parsed the 56 controls
our own documents claim,

00:06:09.075 --> 00:06:14.310
switched 48 of them off one at a time,
and required a green test to go red.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.760
56 of 56 probes were caught and none survived.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.460
Along the way it found a live path traversal,

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:24.110
a way to read files a
server should never serve,

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:25.920
that thirteen green tests had missed.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:30.620
Which brings us back to our
box, and to the pricing gate.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:33.040
With it on, our box gets no card.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:36.140
Before proposing anything,
the agent prices the action:

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:39.260
the chance of a save,
times what a save is worth,

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:43.670
against the cost, and when
that comes out below the cost,

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:45.270
the action is carried as advice.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.700
The agent priced its own actions

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:50.400
and, awkwardly for us, declined most of them.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:56.330
On the same 500 scenarios,
expedites fall from 173 to 29,

00:06:56.330 --> 00:07:04.720
and spend per rollover avoided
from $60,835 to $19,745.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:07.360
That sweep is a simulator.

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:09.740
Does this hold up at volume?

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:14.320
Ten thousand episodes ran with faults
injected into a quarter of them;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:18.010
the slowest one in a hundred
took 21.7 milliseconds,

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:20.770
and nothing was written without an approval.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:27.990
Each email costs the local model 4,689.7
tokens, priced at zero on a laptop.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:31.950
Deny by default has a
cost, and we modelled it:

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:36.100
one officer at half availability
lets 35.6% of cards expire,

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:39.290
and two officers bring that to 5.0%.

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:42.730
No desk has been staffed yet,
so treat that as a model.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:46.940
So whose money is this?

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:52.770
With the gate off, our first
build lost the chain $2.4M a year.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:56.020
With the gate on, and
after paying for the desk,

00:07:56.020 --> 00:08:02.130
the chain nets $87,215 a year,
positive with probability 0.683.

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:08.007
The shipper and the carrier gain, and
PSA's own line sits at minus $1.3M.

00:08:08.007 --> 00:08:09.790
The reason is a tariff assumption:

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:14.760
in the model a rolled box is
billable storage at $20 per box-day,

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.120
so a saved box is a charge
PSA no longer collects.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:20.935
A box inside its free time is not billed,

00:08:20.935 --> 00:08:23.040
so that input is the first to replace.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:29.290
PSA's case is commercial: the
expedite funded by whoever gains,

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:32.800
or the recovery priced into Assured Port Time.

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:35.890
Two limits, before the ask.

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:37.960
The sweep is a simulator grading itself;

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:41.990
an independent oracle agrees
with it 320 of 320 times,

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:44.880
which narrows that objection
without closing it.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:50.440
And our simulator hands the structured
feed the fact 85% of the time,

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:55.290
the opposite of the recorded
days, so we publish both.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:59.580
What we ask of PSA follows:
anonymised connection outcomes;

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:02.230
interface documents for PORTNET

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:07.100
and the Service Allocation Tool;
and PSA's own alert taxonomy.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:09.430
The first deployment would be advisory.

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:10.630
And our box?

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:13.360
Still on the board, at 101
minutes, with the clock running.
